  "full-article-text": "<p id=\"\">When you delegate your <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-are-the-xrd-and-exrd-tokens\"><strong id=\"\">XRD</strong></a> <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-a-token\"><strong id=\"\">tokens</strong></a> to a <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-a-validator-node-2\"><strong id=\"\">validator node</strong></a>, those tokens enter a special “staked” state enforced by the <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-the-radix-protocol\"><strong id=\"\">Radix protocol</strong></a>. You can’t do anything with those tokens while they are in that state. They are “illiquid”.</p><p id=\"\">However, you do still own them, and you can <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/how-to-stake-and-unstake-xrd-in-the-desktop-wallet\"><strong id=\"\">unstake</strong></a> them at any time, after an unstaking delay period required for <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-do-security-safety-and-liveness-mean\"><strong id=\"\">security</strong></a>.&nbsp;</p><p id=\"\">Your tokens are never under the control of the node you stake to. Delegating tokens is just a way of “voting” for which node-runners you want to be in the <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-a-validator-set\"><strong id=\"\">validator set</strong></a>.</p><p id=\"\"><strong id=\"\">Further reading:</strong></p><ul id=\"\"><li id=\"\"><a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/start-here-radix-staking-introduction\"><strong id=\"\">Start Here! Radix Staking Introduction</strong></a></li><li id=\"\"><a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/how-do-xrd-emissions-work\"><strong id=\"\">How do XRD emissions work?</strong></a></li><li id=\"\"><a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/how-to-stake-and-unstake-xrd-in-the-desktop-wallet\"><strong id=\"\">How to Stake and Unstake XRD in the Desktop Wallet</strong></a><br></li></ul>",
